 Detection uses full-resolution captures. The small live view displays the latest inspected screenshot and pink candidate boxes; it is a monitor, not the detector's input resolution. Covered or minimized gameplay cannot be inspected.
 
 ## Gem Sniping
+
+### ADB input (v25, live BlueStacks validation pending)
+
+**Sniping settings → Input method → ADB** sends screenshots, taps and swipes through the selected Android device. It does not move the PC cursor, send Windows keys or bring BlueStacks to the foreground. Mouse mode remains the default for existing settings. Only Gem Sniping uses ADB; the Map overlay and ordinary Gem Watch continue using the visible Windows desktop.
+
+1. Close the old Loong Lens app. For this source package, launch `RUN_LOONG_LENS.bat` with 64-bit Python 3.12 installed; it prepares its own dependencies. Alternatively build the Windows EXE as described under Development. An older EXE will not contain this change.
+2. Enable **Android Debug Bridge** under BlueStacks **Settings → Advanced**. Use the port shown for the instance running your game. In Sniping settings choose **ADB**, enter that exact address (for example `127.0.0.1:5555`), and optionally choose the ADB executable. The app looks for `BlueStacks_nxt/HD-Adb.exe` under Program Files and then `adb` on PATH. You can use Google's Android SDK platform-tools instead. No ADB binary is downloaded or bundled with this package.
+3. Open the game in portrait orientation. With BlueStacks visible for this one-time calibration, press **Pick game area**. Click the top-left and bottom-right of the Android display inside the BlueStacks window, excluding Windows title bar, side toolbar and emulator bottom controls. This preserves the existing 570×1029 reference geometry used by map recognition and HUD guards. If Android navigation bars are included in the device screenshot, include their corresponding image area. Recalibrate after changing the emulator layout or resolution.
+4. Use **Test connection / preview**. This reads device screenshots without sending game input. Check that the game is placed correctly; use **Pick on screenshot** for the minimap and large-map Back button if needed. Save settings. ADB uses the selected address only; it does not auto-select among connected devices. Non-loopback serials must already be connected via your ADB installation.
+5. Start a single **Snipe** pass first. Switch to another desktop app and confirm that map opening, camera checks, screenshots and the final gem tap work correctly. Then try **Loop Sniping**. Keep BlueStacks running and the game active inside Android; some BlueStacks configurations stop rendering when minimized. Covering the desktop window does not cover ADB screenshots.
+
+Route order, saved camera adjustments, extra views, detector confirmation, screenshot/history cooldowns, cumulative timing and repeat/resume behavior are shared with mouse mode. Connection errors never switch to mouse input. Changing input settings clears cached map alignment while retaining processed checks; use Reset checks for a fresh pass. A detected Android app change, invalid screenshot, landscape orientation or resolution change stops input.
+
+F10 prevents subsequent commands and interrupts a waiting local ADB process. A gesture already delivered to Android can finish; swipe durations are bounded to 350–850 ms. Android swipes do not provide the mouse backend's stationary endpoint hold, so inertia, pan speed, screenshot latency and live detector performance need testing on your instance. The current fast 1.2-second inspection interval may need increasing in Sniping settings if ADB capture is slow.
+
+Linux validation covers simulated ADB responses and the real Tk settings flow. It does not establish live BlueStacks compatibility, background/minimized rendering, Android input permission or Windows executable behavior.
+
+ADB captures retain native image detail for gem detection and screenshot history, padded into the existing reference layout. The route's alignment stage still normalizes its own frames to 570×1029. No Windows desktop pixels are used in ADB mode.
+
+### Route and controls
 
 This replaces the former Live patrol tab. The separate Patrol preview tab and its simulation controls have been removed; screenshot-based engine and UI checks remain in the development tools.
 
